Ashantini Chelliah Web Developer 
Project Overview
This project is a personal portfolio website created as part of Project 1 for my web development course.

View live site: https://ashax01.github.io/milestone1/

The aim of the website is to provide a simple and professional space where I can introduce myself, show example projects, and demonstrate the skills I have learned so far using HTML and CSS.

The website is designed to be clear, easy to navigate, and responsive on different screen sizes.

User Experience (UX)
Target Audience
The main users of this site are:

Potential employers
Tutors and assessors
Anyone wanting to view my beginner web development work
User Goals
Users should be able to:

Understand who I am quickly
View my example projects
Navigate between pages easily
Access the site on mobile or desktop without layout issues

User Stories

As a visitor, I want to land on a homepage that clearly explains what the website is.
This is achieved through:
- A clear introduction on the homepage
- An explanation of the website's purpose
- Easy-to-read content and headings

As a user, I want to move between pages using a navigation menu.
This is achieved through:
- A navigation bar displayed on every page
- Links to the Home, About and Portfolio pages
- Consistent navigation throughout the website

As an assessor, I want to see that the site uses clean HTML and CSS structure.
This is achieved through:
- Semantic HTML elements
- An external CSS stylesheet
- Well-organised and readable code

As a recruiter, I want to view projects in a simple portfolio layout.
This is achieved through:
- A dedicated portfolio page
- Project cards displaying project information
- A responsive layout that works across different screen sizes


Design Choices

The website was designed to be simple, professional and easy to navigate.
A dark navigation bar was used to create contrast and improve visibility throughout.
A light background was chosen to keep the site simple and accessible.
Clear headings and spacing were used to separate content and improve readability for all users.
The portfolio page uses a card-based layout to present projects in an organised way.
Inspiration for the layout and structure was taken from:
- MDN Web Docs
- Awwwards portfolio examples


Features

- Multi-page website (Home, About, Portfolio)
- Navigation menu across all pages
- Responsive layout using flexbox
- Simple project card design
- Footer included on every page

Technologies Used

- HTML5 (semantic page structure)
- CSS3 (styling and layout)
- Visual Studio Code (development environment)
- Git and GitHub (version control and deployment)

Testing
Testing was completed manually throughout development.
The following checks were carried out:
- Opened all pages in Chrome
- Checked navigation links worked correctly
- Resized the browser window to confirm responsiveness
- Ensured text remained readable on smaller screens
- Confirmed images scaled correctly across devices
- Tested page layouts on desktop and mobile viewports

Validation

HTML Validation
The HTML pages were tested using the W3C Validator.
During validation, a few small issues were identified including:

- Incorrect charset formatting
- A typo in the stylesheet link attribute
These were corrected and all pages now pass validation successfully.
CSS Validation
The CSS file was tested using the W3C Jigsaw Validator.
No validation errors were found.


Bugs and Fixes
During development, some issues occurred:

Navigation spacing was uneven at first.

Fix:
Flexbox alignment was adjusted to create consistent spacing.

Images did not scale properly on mobile devices.

Fix:
Responsive image styling was implemented using CSS.

Deployment
The website was deployed using GitHub Pages.

Steps:

Push project to GitHub repository
Go to Settings → Pages
Select Main Branch and save
The site becomes available at the GitHub Pages link


